WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:07.270
Murder; call me old fashioned but I’m not
a fan, mainly for moral reasons but also,

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:08.730
it’s just so messy.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:14.089
Thankfully, the law agrees with me and detectives
have become ever more efficient at tracking

00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:18.260
down exactly who is responsible for a particular
crime.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:22.289
The various techniques have become incredibly
advanced and now involve some of the most

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:27.359
cutting edge science so it’s much harder
to convict people on good old fashioned reasons

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:30.310
such as:
“He has a shifty face” or “She just

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:31.730
looks like the type”.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:36.090
No body part is safe from forensics these
days, and it’s not just a dribbling obsessive

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:41.420
compulsive with dandruff that leaves behind
saliva, fingerprints and skin, it’s all

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.420
of us.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.010
So what are the various techniques that modern
police put to use?

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:53.920
Today we learn; how to catch a killer.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.900
Although the Chinese used fingerprints in
the 700s to identify certain documents and

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.140
sculptures, it wasn’t until the end of the
19th century that forensic science really

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.150
took off.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:10.120
The fingerprint was star of the show for many
years, since Sir William Herschel started

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:15.920
using it in India as a way to stop forged
signatures from the 1860s onward.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:21.460
In 1911, Thomas Jennings was the first person
to be convicted of murder on the basis of

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.850
his fingerprints, with a number of experts
on hand to give evidence.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:31.770
And fingerprints replaced Alphonse Bertillon’s
identification method called anthropometry,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.390
which was a set of body measurements, found
to be far more reliable as a way to ID people

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.960
than using early photographs or just asking
“are you Dave?

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:40.960
No?

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.590
Okay, I guess you’re free to go”.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:50.810
Strange though it seems, ballistic fingerprinting
actually started much earlier than fingerprinting,

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:55.990
where an individual bullet was identified
by its shape, not its fingers, in case some

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.320
of you were confused.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:03.270
In 1835, the British police matched a bullet
in a victim to a bullet making mold in the

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.390
killer’s house.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:09.599
Unfortunately, we quickly moved away from
mama’s home-recipe bullets and mass production

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:16.150
prevented this part of forensics, until Calvin
Goddard invented the comparison microscope

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.040
in 1925.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:24.189
The first real test of bullet identification
was after the 1929 St Valentine’s day massacre

00:02:24.189 --> 00:02:30.719
when Goddard proved that it wasn’t carried
out by rogue cops but was in fact Al Capone’s

00:02:30.719 --> 00:02:31.719
henchmen.

00:02:31.719 --> 00:02:33.120
How romantic.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:39.549
The next big jump was when British scientist
Alec Jeffreys pioneered DNA profiling in,

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:42.689
appropriately, the year 1984.

00:02:42.689 --> 00:02:46.430
Big Brother isn’t just watching you, he’s
also collecting your skin.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:51.980
Of course, since the dawn of civilization,
the most important part of any crime is the

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:56.519
truth; who’s telling it, who’s hiding
it, and what was Colonel Mustard doing with

00:02:56.519 --> 00:02:58.459
it in the Billiard room?

00:02:58.459 --> 00:03:03.689
In China, three thousand years ago, suspects
were forced to fill their mouths with dried

00:03:03.689 --> 00:03:07.739
rice and, after a short while, spit it out
again.

00:03:07.739 --> 00:03:13.849
Anyone who’s rice was still dry was found
guilty of lying, since fear and anxiety were

00:03:13.849 --> 00:03:16.279
known to reduce saliva.

00:03:16.279 --> 00:03:20.550
The first lie detection device that was based
on actual science, rather than some sort of

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:25.239
magic demon stick, was Lombrosso’s Glove,
which measured blood pressure.

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:30.609
This eventually morphed into the polygraph
we know today, you know the machine that everyday

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:35.790
answers the question for millions of avid
TV viewers, “who was the father?”

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:41.359
It measures respiratory rate, blood pressure
changes, and changes in galvanic skin response,

00:03:41.359 --> 00:03:44.590
which is the bioelectric reactivity of the
skin.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:49.459
Of course, no polygraph is completely reliable
since the responses they look for are not

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:54.120
unique to lying, they can be caused by simply
being nervous.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:59.040
Understanding the way a killer thinks and
behaves has become a huge part of solving

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.449
cases, and providing the plots for a never
ending sea of TV detective shows.

00:04:03.449 --> 00:04:09.010
The birth of forensic psychology probably
has its roots in the notorious Jack the Ripper

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:13.819
mystery, when an unknown killer terrified
London with a series of bloody murders.

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:19.930
Lacking anything concrete, they tried to build
an image of who their killer was, theorizing

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:25.509
as to whether his motivations were madness,
sexual, religious or a delightful cocktail

00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:26.509
of all three.

00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:30.780
But the large proportion of forensic psychology
is not trying to work out what kind of cereal

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.039
a mass murderer eats for breakfast, but more
on deciding the mental health of people that

00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:35.250
are going to stand trial.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:41.120
The insanity defense is a legal classic and
goes all the way back to the Code of Hammurabi,

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.949
in ancient Mesopotamia.

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:48.669
The Louvre in Paris houses a seven and a half
foot stone slab, inscribed with much of the

00:04:48.669 --> 00:04:52.310
law set by Hammurabi, the sixth king of Babylon.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:56.020
It includes mercy for the insane and also
gave us the famous “eye for an eye”.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.290
It also says that you are liable for the lost
crops if your dam leaks, which, speaking as

00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:58.430
one of many responsible dam owners among us,
I think is often overlooked in modern society.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:04.180
Some forensic methods are a little more unusual,
and no I’m not talking about using a large

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:09.150
woman called Ivanka to talk to the dead and
tell us what Elvis was really up to the day

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:10.150
JFK died.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:15.110
One thing you don’t hear often is, thank
goodness for nuclear bomb testing.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:21.090
But using the levels of Carbon 14 in human
tissue to get an accurate age reading, has

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:26.670
helped a number of cold cases, all thanks
to the atmospheric nukes we detonated during

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.330
the cold war.

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:34.080
This caused a noticeable global spike in carbon
fourteen, which is a radioactive form of carbon

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.810
that does occur naturally but we doubled the
level during these tests.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:44.720
Since then it’s been declining so you can
map the amount in tissue to approximate dates

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.530
of global levels.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:53.319
Meaning we can detect exactly when someone
died just by their carbon-14 levels.

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:58.310
Sometimes though, you don’t need science,
you just need good old fashioned boredom.

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:04.180
Jim Scharf and Dave Heitzman, two detectives
in Washington state, designed a set of playing

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:10.330
cards to give out to bored prisoners, printed
with pictures of cold case victims.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:15.500
Their logic was that the cards would get around
and some prisoner with a grudge, or looking

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.819
to reduce their sentence, might give up a
killer.

00:06:18.819 --> 00:06:19.849
And it worked.

00:06:19.849 --> 00:06:27.590
In 2011, the “get-into-jail-free” cards
produced a conviction for the 1979 murder

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:29.360
of Susan Schwartz.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.879
And what about the future?

00:06:31.879 --> 00:06:36.610
I’m not talking about a Minority Report
ability to stop murders before they happen,

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:40.580
but what tech do we have in the works right
now?

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:44.919
For lie detecting, it would obviously be a
great help to bypass all that obstructive

00:06:44.919 --> 00:06:49.180
skin and facial expression nonsense and get
right into the brain.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:56.050
A functional MRI scan is thought to have a
97% accuracy level for lie detection since

00:06:56.050 --> 00:07:01.050
it shows the increased blood flow to the brain,
required to help you create an untrue story.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:06.499
Unfortunately, it can’t be used on politicians
as they are often shielded from the rays by

00:07:06.499 --> 00:07:07.740
their unwieldy comb-overs.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:14.280
Rumour has it one particular politician has
developed an impenetrable golden mesh of hair

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.692
that enables him to lie with impunity.

00:07:15.692 --> 00:07:22.550
And as for DNA, well gentlemen, I have some
bad news for us.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:27.750
It’s now possible to have a good guess at
a man’s surname, just by looking at his

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:28.870
Y chromosome.

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:34.330
This male chromosome has been passed down
fairly unchanged from generation to generation

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:40.780
so by building a database of old samples with
names we know, the police can look for a similar

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:47.069
Y chromosome to yours and it’s almost a
one in four chance that they can guess your

00:07:47.069 --> 00:07:48.069
surname.

00:07:48.069 --> 00:07:49.069
So, if you want to hire a hitman, either make
sure he’s adopted or just called Agent forty…something.

00:07:49.069 --> 00:07:52.730
So I hope this has been useful if you’re
thinking about rounding up a crime-solving

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:58.290
team and, since I’m an equal-opportunities
YouTuber, if you are planning a murder and

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:01.509
this video has helped you, then you are very
welcome.

00:08:01.509 --> 00:08:03.800
But seriously kids don’t try this at home.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:26.370
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

